  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1027 AM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nSunday through Friday:\n\nHeading into Monday morning high pressure is expected to slowly\nmove across the southwestern Great Lakes Region. This broad area\nof high pressure in the northern plains is expected to slow move\ntoward the mid Atlantic by Tuesday bringing a brief period of \nsubsidence to the state. Thus dry weather and generally quiet\nconditions are expected Monday. \n\nRain chances are expected to return Tuesday into Wednesday as\nanother cold front moves across Wisconsin. Moisture will need to\nreturn behind the exiting high pressure to help any rain even\ndevelop.There could be a world where dry air erodes at some of \nthe storms or prevents stronger storms from developing unless \nWAA out ahead of the cold front is robust. Global ensembles \ngenerally put southern Wisconsin on the northern edge of this \ninstability and moisture. An upper level trough is expected to \nswing through Canada and the Great Lakes Region during this \ntime as well which will provide a nice period of lift and maybe \nsome influence of the upper level jet. If all of these \ningredients can come together at the right time and in the right\nplace then there could be some stronger storms and heavy rain. \nThe best timing of rain and storms looks to hold off until \nTuesday evening into Tuesday night. This current run of guidance\nhas come in slightly drier and lower POP chances, however, this\nseems to be a trend over the last couple of days. 12Z guidance \nbeing generally wetter than 00Z guidance. Which has created this\nosculating pattern from update to update. For this reason, \nthere stuck to the average of guidance and kept a healthy sense \nof skepticism. \n\nAs we start to enter into a more meridional flow aloft, high\npressure is expected to return Thursday. This area of high\npressure will bring a brief period of dry weather as the high\nswings from the Northern Plains through the Great Lakes and \ninto the New England States. Beyond this low chances (~20%) for\nrain chances look to return for the weekend. There is a lot of\nuncertainty here as guidance have varying tracks and strengths\nof an upper level trough. Its fairly easy to see these\ndifferences in the deterministic runs, but this is also visible\nin ensembles and cluster analysis. Regardless of rain chances,\ntemperatures look to be fairly consistent through the extended\nwith highs ranging in the mid 70s to low 80s for much of\nsouthern Wisconsin. \n\nPatterson",
